  "content": "# Binary Search and `lower_bound` Explained\n\n## What is Binary Search?\n\nBinary search is an efficient algorithm for finding a target value within a **sorted** list. Instead of checking every element one by one (which would take O(n) time), binary search repeatedly divides the search interval in half:\n\n1. Look at the middle element.\n2. If the middle element equals the target, you're done.\n3. If the target is smaller, discard the right half.\n4. If the target is larger, discard the left half.\n5. Repeat until the target is found or the interval is empty.\n\nThis gives us **O(log n)** time complexity \u2014 dramatically faster than linear search for large lists.\n\n---\n\n## The `lower_bound` Function\n\nThe `lower_bound` function finds the **first index** where the target could be inserted into a sorted list while maintaining order. In other words, it returns the index of the **first element that is greater than or equal to** the target. If all elements are smaller than the target, it returns the length of the list.\n\nThis is essentially what Python's built-in `bisect_left` does.\n\n```python\nfrom typing import List, TypeVar",
